# Phase 18 P18-V01 Push manager accessors source review — 2026-10-21T15:50:48Z

Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`
Code revision reviewed: `659ec260` (`fix(consumer): map push implementation factory setter`)
Pinned Java source: `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.

## Pinned source findings

- `DefaultMQPushConsumerImpl.get/setConsumeMessageService` returns/replaces the
  `ConsumeMessageService` field. Startup installs the concurrent or orderly
  service. `MQClientInstance.consumeMessageDirectly` calls the getter, while
  the public service interface also exposes start/shutdown, pool sizing,
  direct-consume and Push/POP request submission. The setter has no production
  call site in this pinned tree, but it permits external custom-service
  replacement. Rust owner flows cover built-in service behavior but do not
  expose this replacement interface.
- `MQClientInstance` constructs one `ConsumerStatsManager` and returns that
  same instance. Its public methods record pull/consume RT and TPS and return
  `ConsumeStatus`; `DefaultMQPushConsumerImpl` and all four built-in consume
  services retrieve the manager through the runtime factory.
- Rust stores an internal `ConsumerStatsManager` per `PushConsumer`, keyed by
  client/group/topic. It feeds Push Running Info status but exposes neither
  Java's manager identity nor its public mutation methods and does not share
  the manager through `ClientRuntime`.

Updated the Phase 10 matrix to label both raw accessor surfaces `PARTIAL` and
spell out the missing service replacement and shared mutable stats-manager
semantics. `git diff --check` passed. No tests were run for this source-only
review. Both rows remain open P18-V01 work.
